[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: StrictDoc tooling plan backlog

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-PLAN-SCOPED-BACKLOG
TITLE: Each plan carries its own backlog document, and no new node type
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
An item needs to be tracked while living outside its plan's backlog document,
at which point an explicit register node and a Backlogged_In role become the
cheaper answer.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
Every plan directory may hold a 99-backlog document. Anything noticed but not
groomed goes there as an ordinary node at DEPTH sketch. Grooming means moving
the node into a numbered document in the same plan and raising its DEPTH; the
UID never changes, so nothing that cites it breaks.

The plan's ungroomed set is therefore the query DEPTH == sketch scoped to that
plan's directory. Debt remaining when a plan closes is whatever is still at
sketch, and what to do with it is not decided here.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
A dedicated BACKLOG node type was considered and rejected. The existing types
already say what kind of work an item is -- a MECHANISM to build, a DECISION to
make, a SPIKE to run -- and a separate type would throw that away and need
mapping back on grooming. Containment by document needs no grammar change at
all, and DEPTH sketch already means exactly ungroomed.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Deliberately not solved here: a repo-level backlog above plans. That decision
waits for a plan to actually close with debt on it.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-LOG-DONT-SURFACE
TITLE: Incidental findings go to the backlog, not to session output
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The backlog grows faster than it is groomed, which would mean the filter is
letting too much through and the threshold needs restating.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
A model working in this repository may add backlog nodes on its own initiative,
without asking. It should.

Session output raises a finding only when it matters SOON -- it changes the
decision in front of the operator, or it blocks the work in hand. Everything
else, the whole class of "one more thing you should know", is written to the
plan's backlog document and not spoken. A finding that is logged is not lost, so
mentioning it costs attention while logging it costs nothing.

This applies to planning and implementation sessions alike.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The operator's judgment budget is the scarce resource, not disk or tokens.
Surfacing everything spends that budget on things that are not urgent, and the
predictable result is that genuinely urgent findings get skimmed along with the
rest.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-TREEFMT-SDOC
TITLE: Register strictdoc format as the treefmt formatter for sdoc and sgra
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
treefmt currently formats nothing with an sdoc or sgra extension: there is no
catch-all and unmatched files only warn. The canonical formatter for both is
strictdoc format, which is idempotent over this corpus and currently a no-op
because the files were written in canonical form by hand.

Wire it in treefmt.nix so formatting is enforced rather than incidental.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Two things depend on canonical form. Fingerprints are taken over field text, so
an unformatted file hashes differently from the same content formatted. And the
commit gate runs treefmt with fail-on-change, so anything treefmt does not know
about cannot be enforced there.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Watch for two things when doing it. strictdoc format takes a project directory,
not a file list, which is an awkward fit for treefmt's per-file invocation
model. And it mints a document MID on first run for any document lacking one,
which is a content change treefmt would report as a reformat.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SGRA-BODY-COVERAGE
TITLE: Extend the sdoc tree-sitter grammar to cover document-body content
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Measured while implementing SLICE-SDOC-IN-SEMBLE:
checks/strictdoc-grammar-corpus.nix parses strictdoc-project/strictdoc's own
docs/ (15 files, pinned rev 215cda2e) and gets 5/15 clean under the patched
grammar, unchanged from baseline. The 10 failures are unrelated to
DEC-GRAMMAR-PATCH-NOT-FORK's patch (root-rule choice + REVERSE_ROLE) -- they
are document-body / FREETEXT coverage gaps in the upstream grammar itself,
present before and after this patch.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Out of scope for the current patch, which targets exactly the two bugs this
repository's own corpus hit. Worth a dedicated SPIKE to characterize the
FREETEXT gap before deciding whether it is a small follow-up patch or a
reason to look upstream harder.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-LONG-LIVED-BRANCH
TITLE: Work accumulates on one long-lived branch, restacked into pull requests later
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The first restack completes and the branch merges, at which point the next unit
of work chooses its own shape.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
No pull request is opened during development and none is offered. Work
accumulates on one branch until the operator calls a milestone, then it is
restacked into a series of reviewable pull requests.

Two reviews, with different subjects. The stack is reviewed as a whole for
usability, design and flow. The pull requests are reviewed for implementation
choices. Operator acceptance is turned at the second of these, never before.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Commit boundaries are what make the restack legible, which is why worker
sessions commit per unit of work rather than once at the end. And there is no
signature to turn yet: the fingerprints are placeholders until the suspect-link
check exists, so acceptance has nothing to attach to before then.
<<<

[SLICE]
UID: SLICE-RESTACK-TO-PRS
TITLE: Restack the long-lived branch into reviewable pull requests
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
Cut the accumulated branch into a series of pull requests ordered so each is
independently reviewable, and land them.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Slotted as future work so the strategy is tracked rather than remembered. Not
groomed: the cut points depend on what has accumulated by the milestone.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-LONG-LIVED-BRANCH
  ROLE: Governed_By

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-AUTHORED-BY-GUARD
TITLE: Reject an AUTHORED_BY flip on a node already merged to main
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
A static check that fails when AUTHORED_BY changes from llm to human on a node
whose current value in main is llm. Operator override available, since the
operator is the only party entitled to make that flip.

Could run as a commit hook rather than only in the merge gate, if being
pedantic is worth the friction.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
AUTHORED_BY is the operator's key and nothing currently stops an agent turning
it. A skill rule forbids it, which is guidance rather than enforcement.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SEMBLE-TEST-RECIPE
TITLE: Make a customized semble testable without a devenv shell
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Expose a documented way to build and exercise a grammar-customized semble.
Today it takes a hand-written impure expression against the flake, and the
invocation must scrub PYTHONPATH and redirect the cache.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Measured while verifying SLICE-SDOC-IN-SEMBLE. An installed semble exports
PYTHONPATH, and that shadows any other build: pointing at a freshly built
binary silently runs the installed one with the installed mappings. The failure
presents as the new grammar not working, which is a false defect report waiting
to happen. It already caused one.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-CHUNK-LEADING-WHITESPACE
TITLE: Chunks can begin on inter-node whitespace instead of the node header
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
Measured on an 8762-byte document: of 17 AST chunks, roughly five begin on a
blank line between nodes rather than on the node header or field start that
follows. Semble's sibling-merging walk treats whitespace tokens as ordinary
children and folds them into the front of the next group.

Cosmetic. The chunk still covers the right content and search results are
correct; the leading blank lines waste a little of the embedding window and
make a raw chunk read slightly worse.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Logged rather than fixed. Any fix lives upstream in semble's chunker or in a
grammar change that stops emitting whitespace as a sibling, and neither is
worth it for the observed cost. A good candidate to hand to an execution engine
later rather than to spend design attention on now.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-FP-FIELDS-CONFIGURABLE
TITLE: Which fields are contract-bearing is configuration, not a constant
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The Nix option surface for custom fields and workflow mechanics exists and the
operator has reviewed the field selection through it.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
The set of fields a fingerprint covers is a per-project option, not a hardcoded
list in a script. A prototype may hardcode any reasonable selection to exercise
the logic, but that selection is not settled by having been prototyped.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The selection is the whole mechanism: too broad and every wording change
re-suspects everything until sign-off becomes a rubber stamp, too narrow and a
decision going superseded fails to stale its dependents. That trade is the
operator's to make, and it cannot be reviewed sensibly as an argument in prose
-- it needs the typed option surface the rest of this repository's integrations
are reviewed through.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FP-MAIN-LINEAGE
TITLE: Fail safes for fingerprints that reference a state main cannot reconstruct
DEPTH: needs-design
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
A fingerprint is a content hash of a parent's contract fields, not a Git
object, so squashing does not invalidate it. What squashing destroys is the
ability to RECONSTRUCT the accepted state: if a parent hashed to X only on an
intermediate commit that the squash erased, a later suspect report can say the
edge is stale but not what changed.

Two checks:

- Every recorded hash corresponds to a parent state reachable in main.
- No recorded hash is ahead of the branch's common base with main; if one is,
  rebase before accepting, so acceptances are taken against states that will
  survive the squash.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Without these, an acceptance taken mid-branch against a parent that changed
again before merge leaves a dangling reference the moment the branch squashes.
The mechanism still reports the edge as suspect, which is correct, but it loses
the diff that makes the report actionable -- and a report that only says
"something changed" is the one people learn to ignore.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Merge-time discipline may subsume both. If acceptance is taken as the last step
before merge, the recorded hash is by construction the state that becomes main.
Worth checking whether the two checks are then redundant or still needed as a
backstop against acceptances taken earlier in the branch.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: MECH-FP-CHECK
  ROLE: Assumes

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FP-PREMERGE-GC
TITLE: Keep fingerprint lineage during development, collapse it before merge
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
Permit fingerprints referencing states between the branch head and its common
base with main while the branch is in development, then run a specialized
garbage collection before merge that erases the ones the squash is about to
make unreachable.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Full lineage across a multi-commit pull request is genuinely useful while
working -- it is the trace of how a design was reasoned through. It becomes
dangling references the instant the branch squashes. Collecting at the boundary
keeps the value and pays the cost once.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Needs grooming, and possibly a plan revision rather than a mechanism: it may
change how MECH-FP-CHECK reports rather than being a separate step. Not settled
that a garbage collection is the right shape.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: MECH-FP-MAIN-LINEAGE
  ROLE: Assumes

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FP-ACCEPT-READINESS
TITLE: Refuse to sign a fingerprint against a parent that is still moving
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
fp-accept refuses to record a hash for a parent that is not yet stable:
a MECHANISM, SLICE, INVARIANT or SPIKE below interface-settled, or a DECISION
whose STATUS is open.

No new field is needed. DEPTH already distinguishes still-moving from stable,
and STATUS does the same for decisions.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Accepting a contract asserts that no further change is expected. A hash taken
against a node someone is still editing is meaningless: it goes stale the next
day, and the resulting suspect report trains the reader to dismiss suspects.
<<<
PARENT_FP: MECH-FP-CHECK:0000000
NOTES: >>>
Implemented as sdoc_fp.is_ready() (dev/scripts/sdoc_fp.py), the single
readiness predicate dev/scripts/fp-accept.py calls before signing any entry.
Verified against a synthetic fixture covering both refusal branches: a
DECISION with STATUS open and a MECHANISM at DEPTH sketch. Both were
refused with the reason stated; neither was silently skipped.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: MECH-FP-CHECK
  ROLE: Governed_By

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FP-GIT-FLOW-HAZARDS
TITLE: Git operations that change node content before it reaches main
DEPTH: needs-design
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
Squash is SAFE. It preserves the final tree, so a content hash is byte
identical before and after and matches the branch tip. Only the intermediate
commits are lost, which costs diagnostics rather than correctness.

Rebase is the hazard, in two ways:

- Rebasing onto a main that changed a node you depend on makes the parent's
  content genuinely different, so a recorded hash goes stale. This is correct
  behavior arriving at the wrong moment: during a mechanical operation, where
  the reader is not thinking about design and learns to dismiss the report.
- Conflict resolution can produce node content that neither side authored. The
  result is a contract nobody accepted, created silently by a merge.

Restacking is a series of rebases and therefore the second hazard repeatedly.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
None of this bites until a branch reaches main; before that it is local churn
and a re-accept costs nothing. Merging to main is the forcing function, so the
handling is deliberately deferred until the first merge -- by which point the
approach may have changed enough that a solution designed now would be wasted.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
What is NOT yet designed: whether a rebase should re-accept automatically when
the parent's change is confined to fields outside the fingerprint, and how to
detect content produced by conflict resolution rather than by an author. Both
want a real merge to look at rather than speculation.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: MECH-FP-CHECK
  ROLE: Assumes

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FP-FIELD-TUNING
TITLE: Tune the contract field selection once the option surface exists
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
Revisit which fields a fingerprint covers, through the Nix option surface,
after the prototype has been in use. The prototype's hardcoded selection is a
starting point chosen to exercise the logic, not a settled answer.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The option surface is not load bearing for building the detector, so waiting on
it would block work for no gain. But the selection does need the operator's
review eventually, and reviewing it as prose was already rejected.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-FP-FIELDS-CONFIGURABLE
  ROLE: Governed_By

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-FP-ACCEPT-AUTHORITY
TITLE: Who may sign a fingerprint should depend on the grade, not be human-only
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: open
RETIRES_ON: >>>
A real drift event produces enough edges to make the current rule painful,
which is the first evidence of where the line actually belongs.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
Human-only signing is the current rule and is too blunt. If a couple of nodes
change in a way that touches a hundred edges without meaning anything, and both
ends are llm-authored and llm-graded, an agent should be able to re-sign those
without raising them.

The line is authority, not effort. A contract the operator authored or accepted
escalates to the operator. One that has only ever been llm-graded does not.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The failure mode of human-only is not that it is unsafe, it is that it floods.
A queue of a hundred no-impact acceptances trains the operator to approve
without reading, which is the same rubber stamp the rule exists to prevent,
arrived at from the other direction.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Unsettled: whether the deciding field is the PARENT's authority, the CHILD's,
or either end being human. The parent owns the contract that moved; the child
owns the assumption that may no longer hold. Both readings are defensible and
the answer probably wants a real drift event to look at.

AUTHORED_BY already carries the distinction, so this likely needs no new field.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-RUNTIME-WRITE-GUARD
TITLE: Enforce field ownership at the runtime rather than in a document
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
Guard operator-owned fields where the model actually acts, not in guidance it
has to have read. A tool-call hook that inspects an edit for a change to a
guarded field and fails loudly back to the model is the cheap version, and it
works whether the model edits directly or through a script it wrote.

Not exhaustive, and heavier options are probably bad ideas: a filesystem layer
that makes guarded fields unwritable was considered and is recorded only so it
is not reinvented enthusiastically later.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Demonstrated rather than hypothetical. A hard rule in the authoring skill did
not prevent an agent from signing five contracts, because a rule in a document
is guidance and nothing checks it. The same gap covers AUTHORED_BY, which
MECH-AUTHORED-BY-GUARD already tracks from the static side; this is the runtime
side of the same problem.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-AGENT-IDENTITY-KEYS
TITLE: Give agents their own signing identity
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
Per-agent SSH keys, so a commit made by a model is attributable to that model
rather than to the operator whose machine it ran on.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Identity is the one thing that must not go fuzzy, and today it is: an agent
commits under the operator's name, which is what made five agent-written
signatures forensically indistinguishable from real ones. Every guard
downstream of this -- fingerprint acceptance, AUTHORED_BY, any future audit --
rests on being able to tell who acted, and none of them can while the answer is
always the operator.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Probably belongs to the beads work rather than here, and is likely to get lost
there. Tracked in this plan so it survives, not because this is its home.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-NO-DELETES-YET
TITLE: Do not delete nodes until the immutability mechanics are settled
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The retention and immutability question settles: whether a node is deleted with
Git history as the record, or is immutable outside explicit state changes.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
Supersede, do not delete. Until the record model is chosen, a removed node is
an unrecoverable choice made by whoever happened to be editing.

Known violation, logged rather than reverted: SLICE-SEMBLE-SDOC was deleted
during deduplication, having been written twice in two places. Restoring it
would recreate the duplicate it was removed to fix, so grooming should rule on
whether to reinstate it as superseded or accept the deletion.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The two candidate models -- delete with Git history as the record, versus
immutable outside state changes -- differ in what a deletion costs. Deleting
under the first is routine; under the second it destroys the record. Choosing
by accident, one edit at a time, is how the answer gets made without anyone
deciding it.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-GROOM-CONTRADICTION-CHECK
TITLE: Grooming must surface contradictions, not just missing edges
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
When a node is groomed, check whether an edge is missing that should exist, and
whether an existing edge now contradicts what the node says. Surface the
collision rather than silently resolving it.

A live example to test against: DEC-FP-ACCEPT-AUTHORITY holds that human-only
signing is too blunt and should key on grade. At least three places currently
assert human-only outright -- the authoring skill's hard rule, the acceptance
language in DEC-LONG-LIVED-BRANCH, and the two-keys split recorded around
DEPTH verified. None of them cite DEC-FP-ACCEPT-AUTHORITY and none is edged to
it, so nothing connects the open question to the assertions it contradicts.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
This is the failure the whole system exists to catch, and it is currently
uncaught in its own graph. The author's stated reason for building this is
returning to a conclusion having forgotten the reasoning; a contradiction with
no edge between its two halves is exactly that, made durable.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-TESTS-AS-CONTRACT
TITLE: Flows get tests as a contract once they settle
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
Once a flow's mechanics are settled, it gets tests that stand as its contract.
No regression is acceptable in this area afterwards.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Deliberately after settling, not before. Writing tests against mechanics still
being played with pins the wrong thing and makes changing it feel like
breakage.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-READY-QUERY-GRAPH-REUSE
TITLE: MECH-READY-QUERY's closure walk can reuse cycle-check's graph builder
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
dev/scripts/cycle-check.py builds a UID -> [(target_uid, role)] adjacency map
over every Parent-type relation via sdoc_fp.build_uid_index and a small
RELATIONS walk. MECH-READY-QUERY needs the same shape of graph to walk a
slice's transitive contract-dependency closure and test each node's DEPTH /
STATUS against readiness. Building it should be factored into sdoc_fp.py (or
a shared graph module) once MECH-READY-QUERY is implemented, rather than
reimplemented.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Noticed while implementing SLICE-GRAPH-CHECKS: MECH-CYCLE-CHECK and
MECH-READY-QUERY both start from "walk the relation graph from a node," and
cycle-check.py already has a tested version of that walk (verified against
synthetic fixtures, see MECH-CYCLE-CHECK's NOTES). Not acted on now --
MECH-READY-QUERY is still needs-design and this is an implementation detail
for whoever designs it, not a reason to touch it while implementing an
unrelated slice.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-TUI-BOARD-SUBJECT
TITLE: Whether the board renders plans or work
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: open
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The TUI's backend is specified against a real data layer rather than fixtures.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
The kanban prototype's data model is the earlier overlay's -- TASK, PLAN,
MILESTONE, a work STATUS spanning open through done, and blocking edges. The
grammar in use has none of those: no work-status field anywhere, no milestone,
and node types that describe design rather than work.

A board over the PLAN would reintroduce the work store the plan deliberately
does not keep. A board over the execution engine, fed by the plan at ingest, is
the shape that survives MECH-PLAN-CHANGE-KINDS.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Recorded now because the divergence is invisible while the backend is fixtures
and expensive once it is not. The prototype labels itself honestly -- fixture
backend, no SDoc writes -- so nothing is wrong yet; this is only the fork that
gets chosen when the fixtures are replaced.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Not a criticism of the prototype, which is deliberately unspecced at this
layer. The operator raised the same gap independently.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SDOC-CONFIG-HOOK-IMPORTABILITY
TITLE: strictdoc_config.py must put itself on sys.path before defining a hook class
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
On nixpkgs strictdoc 0.27.1, any Format or StrictDocPlugin subclass defined in
strictdoc_config.py crashes every parallelised command -- export with the
default parallelizer, format, manage new, manage auto-uid -- with
ModuleNotFoundError: strictdoc_config in the forkserver workers.
helpers/module.py loads the config by file path without touching sys.path, and
the workers unpickle a ProjectConfig that carries the hook instance. Only
export --no-parallelization survives.

Measured fix: sys.path.insert(0, dirname(abspath(__file__))) as the config's
first statement. All four commands then work, for inline hook classes and for
sibling modules alike. Put the line in the config and in the skill's gotcha
list; drop it once nixpkgs lands >= 0.28.1, whose helpers/module.py does it.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Measured by SPIKE-EXECUTABLE-RULES. Any plugin or Format-seam guard is dead on
arrival without it, and the failure presents as a crash in a worker rather than
as a config error. The upstream fix is the same line moved into the tool, so a
version bump is a convenience here, not a capability.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SDOC-LEAN-FORMATS
TITLE: Pin formats to SDoc and JSON in strictdoc_config.py
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
ProjectConfig.default_formats() imports the HTML, Excel, PDF and tree-map
stack and costs about 0.7 s of every CLI invocation; the forkserver
parallelizer adds about 1.4 s more. Passing formats=[SDocFormat(),
JSONFormat()] cuts export from 3.2 to 1.24 s, export --no-parallelization
from 1.79 to 0.85 s, format from 3.3 to 1.15 s and manage new from 3.2 to
1.15 s, with the JSON export byte-identical for all 65 nodes.

Two consequences to handle in the same change. Markdown ingestion stops, so
exclude_doc_paths and the config docstring saying both globs are required go
stale. And export --formats=html then exits 0 while writing nothing, because
argparse still accepts the handle and no registered Format claims it -- a
false green. Pin the formats only in a config the wrapper and CI read, or keep
HTMLFormat and pay the 0.6 s.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The traceability index build is 3 ms; the cost of every strictdoc command is
startup, config construction and the parallelizer. This is the single largest
lever on the skill loop and on every validation tier, and it needs no package
change.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-READINESS-PREDICATE-RECONCILE
TITLE: One readiness predicate, consumed by every script that asks
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Five definitions of ready exist and no two agree on rejected, superseded and
suspect. sdoc_fp.is_ready refuses only STATUS open, so fp-accept signs against
a rejected or superseded parent. status.py closure_verdict blocks superseded
and passes rejected, and never calls fp-check, so no-suspect-edge is not
applied. The skill, MECH-READY-QUERY and DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY each word
it a third way. SLICE-SDOC-IN-SEMBLE is verified while status.py reports it
blocked on DEC-ARCH-COLOCATION being open.

Latent today: zero rejected decisions and one superseded decision with no
dependents. Decide the rule, then one predicate in sdoc_fp consumed by both
scripts and by MECH-READY-QUERY when it lands.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Two executables implementing two readings of one rule is exactly the
document-borne drift INV-GUARDS-MUST-EXECUTE names; the disagreement is
invisible until the first rejected decision acquires a dependent.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY
  ROLE: Governed_By

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-RELATION-TARGET-TYPE-LINT
TITLE: Lint relation target types, which the parser never checks
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
strictdoc validates a relation's ROLE against the source element's grammar
only; the target's element type is never checked. Measured: a MECHANISM
Governed_By a SPIKE exports clean. The skill's role table is prose.

The live graph already carries two Governed_By edges whose target is not a
DECISION -- MECH-FP-ACCEPT-READINESS to MECH-FP-CHECK, and
SPIKE-CONVERSION-SIGNAL to SLICE-FIRST-CONVERSION -- plus five MECHANISM
Assumes MECHANISM edges the skill's table gives no target for, and
Serialized_By has no stated target at all. Decide the Assumes and
Serialized_By rules, fix or accept the two edges, then wire the lint. A lint
wired first goes red on day one.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The parser proves an edge resolves, never that it points at the kind of node
the role promises. Corpus first, gate second is this repo's own practice from
SLICE-FP-DETECTOR.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: MECH-CYCLE-CHECK
  ROLE: Assumes

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-PLAN-REFS-CHECK
TITLE: Check INV-NO-EXTERNAL-PLAN-REFS over the export
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
The invariant is violated twice today: MECH-RENDER-CACHE and
MECH-CORPUS-EDGE-MAP, both in the beads-migration plan, depend into the
strictdoc-tooling plan. The JSON export carries no document path -- a
document is TITLE, GRAMMAR and NODES -- so the check needs a UID-to-file map,
the same gap fp-accept works around by grepping UID lines in the source tree.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Predicted by the invariant's own NOTES and now measured. Cheap once the map
exists, and the map is needed by the wrapper anyway.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: INV-NO-EXTERNAL-PLAN-REFS
